\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The retained Gaussian marginal}
\date{2026-10-17}

\p{Use the orthogonal sum #{E=X\mathbin\perp Y}, positive precision
#{Q}, effective precision #{S} and exact scalar #{c_C} of
[Gaussian elimination](fdip-000D). Let #{\pi=p_X:E\to X} and let
#{\mu_Q,\mu_S} be the independently defined
\vocabk{Gaussian probability laws}{fdip-0005}. Then
##{Z_Q=c_CZ_S,\qquad \pi_*\mu_Q=\mu_S.}
Thus the \newvocab{retained marginal law} is #{\mu_S}: for every
Borel #{D\subseteq X}, #{\mu_Q(\pi^{-1}D)=\mu_S(D)}. Here
pushforward assigns #{D} the mass of its inverse image, extending
the [finite pushforward convention](ftip-007S) to these Borel spaces.}
\p{Every real Borel function #{F:X\to\mathbb R} satisfies the
extended nonnegative identity
##{\int_E|F\circ\pi|\,d\mu_Q
       =\int_X|F|\,d\mu_S\ \in[0,\infty].}
Consequently #{F\in L^1(\mu_S)} if and only if
#{F\circ\pi\in L^1(\mu_Q)}. Under these equivalent conditions,
##{\int_E F\circ\pi\,d\mu_Q=\int_X F\,d\mu_S.}
We use actual Borel functions; #{L^1} here denotes their integrable
vector space. It is [not asserted to be an algebra](fdip-0030).}

\proof{
\p{The projection is linear, hence continuous and Borel. The weights
are continuous and nonnegative, and the [orthogonal product volumes](fdip-002Q)
are sigma-finite. Nonnegative Tonelli and the fiber formula give
##{\begin{aligned}
Z_Q&=\int_X\int_Y w_Q(J(x,y))\,d\lambda_Y(y)\,d\lambda_X(x)\\
 &=c_C\int_X e^{-h_X(x,Sx)/2}\,d\lambda_X(x)\\
 &=c_CZ_S.
\end{aligned}}
All three factors are positive and finite by [normalization](fdip-002T).
This also agrees with #{\det Q=\det C\det S}.}
\p{For a Borel set #{D}, repeat Tonelli with the nonnegative factor
#{\mathbf1_D(x)}. Division by #{Z_Q=c_CZ_S} gives
##{\begin{aligned}
\mu_Q(\pi^{-1}D)
 &=\frac{c_C}{Z_Q}\int_D e^{-h_X(x,Sx)/2}\,d\lambda_X(x)\\
 &=\mu_S(D).
\end{aligned}}
This proves equality of measures without inferring it from second moments.}
\p{Repeat the same calculation with #{|F(x)|}. Nonnegative Tonelli
allows the value infinity, so it proves the asserted absolute-integral
identity before any integrability assumption. If these absolute
integrals are finite, signed Fubini applies. Equivalently, repeat the
nonnegative calculation for #{F^+} and #{F^-} and subtract their
finite integrals. This proves the signed expectation equality.}
}

\p{For a finite real vector space #{W}, write #{\mathcal P(W)} for
its [intrinsic polynomial function algebra](fdip-002V), generated
by linear functionals. The actual map on observables is
##{\begin{aligned}
\pi^*:\mathcal P(X)&\longrightarrow\mathcal P(E),\\
P&\longmapsto P\circ\pi.
\end{aligned}}
It is an injective unital real algebra homomorphism preserving the
identity involutions. For the [Gaussian polynomial states](fdip-002X),
##{\omega_S=\omega_Q\circ\pi^*.}
The field projection goes #{E\to X}; this observable inclusion goes
in the opposite direction.}
\proof{
\p{A linear functional on #{X} pulls back to a linear functional on
#{E}, so composition preserves the polynomial algebras. Pointwise
evaluation proves preservation of sums, products, real scalar
multiples and the constant one. Both involutions are the identity.
Since #{\pi i_X=I_X}, equality #{P\circ\pi=R\circ\pi} composed
with #{i_X} implies #{P=R}. This is the invariant form of
[polynomial pullback](fdip-002A), with the actual section #{i_X}.}
\p{Every polynomial, product and square is absolutely integrable by
\ref{fdip-002W}. Thus #{\omega_Q(P)=\int P\,d\mu_Q} and
#{\omega_S(P)=\int P\,d\mu_S} are independently defined real
Hermitian positive normalized functionals: they are linear, take
one to one, respect the identity star and take #{P^*P=P^2} to a
nonnegative number. Apply the expectation identity to each #{P} to
obtain #{\omega_S(P)=\omega_Q(\pi^*P)}. Abstract
[state pullback](fdip-0027) guarantees that the right side is a
state; the integral calculation identifies which Gaussian state it is.
No conditional expectation, partial trace or state on field
endomorphisms has been introduced.}
}

\p{The inverse-compression and covariance identities are
##{\begin{aligned}
p_XQ^{-1}i_X&=S^{-1},\\
\operatorname{Cov}_{\mu_Q}(\ell_j\circ\pi,\ell_k\circ\pi)
 &=h_X(j,S^{-1}k),
\end{aligned}}
where #{\ell_j(x)=h_X(j,x)}. The means vanish. In counting
coordinates this is #{(Q^{-1})_{RR}=S^{-1}}.}
\proof{
\p{To check [inverse compression](fdip-001V) directly, solve
#{QJ(u,v)=i_Xb}. Its lower component gives #{v=-C^{-1}B^\dagger u};
its upper gives #{Su=b}. Hence #{u=S^{-1}b=p_XQ^{-1}i_Xb}.
The existence of #{C^{-1}} uses positivity, not merely invertibility
of the whole #{Q}.}
\p{Orthogonality gives #{\ell_j\pi(z)=h_E(i_Xj,z)}.
[Inverse-precision covariance](fdip-000B) and
#{i_X^\dagger=p_X} then give the claimed covariance and zero means.
Alternatively use the expectation equality for #{\ell_j,\ell_k}
and their product. These functions are integrable polynomials.
For a nonorthonormal basis, coordinate functionals have their own
Riesz representatives; their covariance array is not an unqualified
array of the inverse operator.}
}

\p{[Fixing the eliminated field](fdip-003A) is a different operation
from averaging it. The conditional-law calculation gives its parameters;
the [four-cycle comparison](fdip-000F) exhibits a difference even when
the fixed value is zero.}
\p{\citet{Appendix A.2, equations (A.5)-(A.6), p. 200}{rasmussen2006gaussian}
gives Gaussian marginal and conditional parameters. The inverse
block formula is in
\citet{Appendix A.3, equation (A.13), p. 202}{rasmussen2006gaussian}.
Its joint parameter is covariance #{\Sigma=Q^{-1}}. Retained
covariance is therefore #{p_X\Sigma i_X=S^{-1}}, not #{S}.}
\p{In orthonormal coordinates, #{\Sigma^{-1}=Q} and
#{\det\Sigma=(\det Q)^{-1}} turn the provider density into
#{Z_Q^{-1}w_Q}; the [density and volume comparison](fdip-000B)
identifies the probability laws. Mathlib's
\leanref{ProbabilityTheory.measurePreserving_restrict₂_multivariateGaussian}
then supports coordinate restriction with covariance #{\Sigma}.
TauCeti's \leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.Probability.map_affine_multivariateGaussian}
supports rectangular affine images with positive-semidefinite
covariance. Apply its linear map as the orthonormal presentation of
#{p_X} and its translation as zero. These concern Gaussian
probability measures, not volume preservation by a projection.}
\p{Mathlib's \leanref{MeasureTheory.integral_prod} supports signed
Fubini under integrability. None of these declarations supplies the
raw factor #{c_C} or removes the preceding absolute-value argument.}
